Subject: [PATCH] main: run crash handler on a sigaltstack so it survives stack-overflow faults
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (stacks on 0001-0015)

prudynt's SIGSEGV/SIGABRT/... handler (crash_signal_handler_extended) writes a
register+backtrace report to /tmp/prudynt_crash.log. But it was installed WITHOUT
an alternate signal stack (no sigaltstack / no SA_ONSTACK), so on a stack-overflow
fault the kernel cannot push the signal frame onto the exhausted stack -> the
handler never runs -> no crash report, and the crash is invisible (exactly what we
saw debugging a startup SIGSEGV: dmesg showed the fault but /tmp/prudynt_crash.log
was never created).

Fix: allocate a small static alternate stack, register it with sigaltstack(), and
add SA_ONSTACK to the handler's sa_flags. Now the handler runs and produces the
diagnostic report regardless of fault type. This is a real, permanent robustness
improvement (not just for this investigation). One hunk in main(); uses
<signal.h> (already included).

Verified: git apply --check + patch -p1 both pass against f4b3228 + 0001-0015.

diff --git a/src/main.cpp b/src/main.cpp
index 2b35d77..e79037a 100644
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -530,12 +530,25 @@ int main(int argc, const char *argv[]) {
 
   // Install crash signal handlers to clean up lock file and collect diagnostics
   // on abnormal termination
+  // DIAGNOSTIC: run the crash handler on a dedicated alternate stack
+  // (SA_ONSTACK). Without this the handler cannot execute when the fault is a
+  // stack overflow (the kernel can't push the signal frame onto an exhausted
+  // stack), so no /tmp/prudynt_crash.log is produced and the crash is invisible.
+  // With an alt stack the handler runs and writes the register/backtrace report
+  // regardless of fault type.
+  static char crash_alt_stack[32768];
+  stack_t crash_ss;
+  crash_ss.ss_sp = crash_alt_stack;
+  crash_ss.ss_size = sizeof(crash_alt_stack);
+  crash_ss.ss_flags = 0;
+  sigaltstack(&crash_ss, nullptr);
+
   struct sigaction sa;
   memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
   sa.sa_sigaction = crash_signal_handler_extended;
   sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
-  sa.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO | SA_RESETHAND; // Get detailed signal info, reset to
-                                           // default after first invocation
+  sa.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO | SA_RESETHAND | SA_ONSTACK; // detailed info; reset to
+                                           // default after first; run on altstack
 
   sigaction(SIGSEGV, &sa, nullptr); // Segmentation fault
   sigaction(SIGABRT, &sa, nullptr); // Abort signal
